# BeHazel'd IIS Deployment Environment Guide

This guide wires the IIS-hosted luxury storefront to the BeHazeld OS backend.

## 1. Website Environment Variables

Set these variables before building/running the Next.js storefront in:

`The Complete Story/BeHazeld_Website/frontend`

### Option A: IIS hosts the website and proxies `/api` to FastAPI

Use this when customers visit `https://behazeld.com` and IIS forwards
`https://behazeld.com/api/...` to the FastAPI backend on port `8000`.

```env
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://behazeld.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_TENANT_ID=b86e1ba8-2d77-4eed-a069-4041504d3717
```

Important: do not set `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://behazeld.com/api`.
The storefront already calls `/api/v1/public/...`; setting `/api` twice would
produce `/api/api/v1/...`. The website adapter also strips one trailing `/api`
as a safety guard, but the clean value is `https://behazeld.com`.

### Option B: Backend runs on a separate API domain

Use this when the backend is exposed separately, for example
`https://api.behazeld.com`.

```env
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://api.behazeld.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_TENANT_ID=b86e1ba8-2d77-4eed-a069-4041504d3717
```

### Option C: Temporary Railway backend

Use this while the backend remains on Railway.

```env
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://the-complete-story-production.up.railway.app
NEXT_PUBLIC_TENANT_ID=b86e1ba8-2d77-4eed-a069-4041504d3717
```

## 2. Backend Environment Variables

Set these for the FastAPI backend process.

```env
APP_ENV=production
APP_NAME=BeHazeld E-Commerce OS
API_V1_PREFIX=/api/v1
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST/DB?sslmode=require
JWT_SECRET_KEY=<long-random-secret>
JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRE_DAYS=7
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=<cloudinary-cloud-name>
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=<cloudinary-api-key>
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=<cloudinary-api-secret>
ALLOWED_ORIGINS_RAW=https://behazeld.com,https://www.behazeld.com
```

If the Admin OS is hosted on a separate domain, add it too:

```env
ALLOWED_ORIGINS_RAW=https://behazeld.com,https://www.behazeld.com,https://admin.behazeld.com
```

For local testing:

```env
ALLOWED_ORIGINS_RAW=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3001,https://behazeld.com,https://www.behazeld.com
```

The backend uses `ALLOWED_ORIGINS_RAW` to configure CORS and allows credentials
because the Admin OS uses httpOnly JWT cookies.

## 3. IIS Reverse Proxy Setup with ARR

Goal:

```text
https://behazeld.com/api/v1/public/{tenant_id}/products
        -> http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/public/{tenant_id}/products
```

### IIS prerequisites

Install:

1. IIS URL Rewrite Module
2. Application Request Routing (ARR)

Then enable proxying:

1. Open IIS Manager.
2. Click the server node.
3. Open **Application Request Routing Cache**.
4. Click **Server Proxy Settings**.
5. Check **Enable proxy**.
6. Apply.

### URL Rewrite rule

Create this rule on the IIS site for `behazeld.com`:

```xml
<rule name="ReverseProxyToFastAPI" stopProcessing="true">
  <match url="^api/(.*)" />
  <action type="Rewrite" url="http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/{R:1}" />
</rule>
```

Explanation:

- Browser calls: `https://behazeld.com/api/v1/public/...`
- IIS matches everything after `/api/`.
- IIS forwards to: `http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/public/...`
- FastAPI receives its real backend path unchanged.

If FastAPI runs on another Windows service/port, update only the target:

```xml
<action type="Rewrite" url="http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/{R:1}" />
```

## 4. Backend Service on Windows/IIS Host

FastAPI should run as a separate process behind IIS, for example:

```powershell
cd "C:\path\to\The Complete Story"
set PYTHONPATH=C:\path\to\The Complete Story\BeHazeld_OS_Backend
python -m uvicorn BeHazeld_OS_Backend.app.main:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000
```

For production, run it as a Windows Service using NSSM, WinSW, or another
service manager so it restarts automatically after server reboot.

## 5. Public API Contract to Test

Replace `<tenant_id>` with:

```text
b86e1ba8-2d77-4eed-a069-4041504d3717
```

Expected public endpoints:

```text
GET https://behazeld.com/api/v1/public/<tenant_id>/categories
GET https://behazeld.com/api/v1/public/<tenant_id>/products
GET https://behazeld.com/api/v1/public/<tenant_id>/products/<product_uuid>
```

The product response should include:

```text
product_code
category_id
image_url
variants[].sku_code
variants[].size_name
variants[].color_name
variants[].color_hex_code
variants[].fabric
variants[].mrp
variants[].selling_price
variants[].stock_count
variants[].is_available
variants[].image_url
```

## 6. Image Pipeline Validation

Product and variant images must be uploaded from the Admin OS.

Flow:

```text
Admin upload -> FastAPI ImageService -> Cloudinary secure_url -> Neon DB image_url -> Storefront renders URL
```

No image file should be manually copied into the website build. The website
renders Cloudinary URLs directly from the public API.

## 7. Go-Live Wiring Checklist

1. Backend `/health` returns `{"status":"ok"}`.
2. Backend `/health/db` returns database connected.
3. IIS `/api/v1/public/<tenant_id>/categories` returns JSON.
4. IIS `/api/v1/public/<tenant_id>/products` returns active products.
5. Product variants include real `size_name`, `color_name`, `stock_count`, and `is_available`.
6. Change a product selling price in Admin OS; refresh the product page and confirm the new price appears.
7. Change a product category in Admin OS; confirm it moves to the correct website collection.
8. Change a size/color in Admin OS; confirm product variant controls update.
9. Upload a product image in Admin OS; confirm the storefront image URL is Cloudinary.
10. Record stock movement to zero; confirm the storefront shows sold out and disables add to bag.
11. Record stock movement above zero; confirm the variant becomes selectable again.
12. Confirm `ALLOWED_ORIGINS_RAW` contains every live IIS/Vercel/Admin origin.
13. Confirm browser devtools has no CORS errors.
14. Confirm no `.env` secrets are committed to GitHub.

